From: =?UTF-8?q?Berkay=20K=C3=B6sebay?= <brkosebay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 00:08:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] drm/imagination: apple: Run jobs on the A9 (S8003)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The A9's firmware is now given geometry, fragment and compute jobs, so the
iPad 5's GPU renders through powervr's UAPI.

How this firmware takes a job differs from the A10's, and the chip does
that part itself (submit(), engine_done() and the other chip functions):

- Channels. Each engine has a channel in the shared memory: a ring of 16
  pointers with five watermarks, two stamps, a timestamp queue and a
  dependency object. A job is a work record the firmware loads the
  engine's registers from and a microsequence it runs for it; a render
  job has a command ahead of the record (a geometry job hands over the
  parameter manager's record, a fragment job waits for its geometry
  job's stamp). An entry of the engine's work ring and the META's
  scheduler register tell the firmware. A job is done when both stamps
  reached its number and all watermarks its end of the pointer ring.
- Work records. The kernel builds them from the Rogue command, the A9
  stream extension and the HWRT dataset: the words Apple's kext sets the
  same for every render, userspace's addresses and program words, and
  the target's geometry, which it derives from width, height and samples
  as the kext does.
- VMs. A VM gets a pair of the firmware's slots with its first job: two
  device-control commands make the firmware load its page-table root.
  When powervr flushes the MMU, the tag of every slot in use changes, and
  the firmware drops the slot's translations when its next job starts.
  A VM that goes leaves its slots on a park VM's tables, because the
  parameter manager still writes through them for a while.
- Parameter manager. Its state for a scene is a PM set: three pages in
  the firmware's GiB, mapped in every VM where the firmware has them and
  protected as the firmware's are, a pool of kernel pages, and the PM's
  own tables, kernel pages mapped protected in the VM. Free lists hold
  no pages on this chip: their size says how large a VM's pool is.
- Two scenes of one VM may be in the firmware, each in its own PM set,
  and a fragment job is submitted right after its geometry job: the
  kernel side of the pipelining in orchard #119. The doorbell is rung
  for every job unless its own stamps show it done, the rule of #125.
- Completion is polled, as on the A10: the interrupt controller's driver
  cannot acknowledge the firmware's interrupt. While a job is waited
  for, the host reads the firmware's memory once a millisecond, which
  the firmware needs to see its own progress.
- A job that does not finish in the scheduler's 5 s ends every job in
  flight; the firmware is stopped, the GPU's power domain goes off for
  at least 10 ms through runtime PM, and the next job boots the firmware
  again. No context is frozen and there is no fault budget.

Ported from Berkay Kösebay's apple-g5p driver (orchard #74 with #119 and
#125): drivers/gpu/apple-g5p/render-user-layout.h, render-user-geometry.h,
render-user-msaa.h, render-user-bind.h, render-user-mmu.h,
render-user-pmset.h, render-user-wake.h, compute-user-layout.h, and
drivers/gpu/drm/apple-g5p/drm-render-v3.inc, drm-render.inc,
drm-compute-v1.inc. Every layout, offset and value is his. Not taken: his
own UAPI, VM and scheduler, the validation of userspace's addresses, his
job logs, switches and alternate paths.

Not run on hardware.

Assisted-by: LLM
